Z||000009||theogre||06-06-2001||08:02 PM||||Well... I think a company like Wilwood probably knows more than all of us put together about what brake fluid works how.... They pretty much hate silicone.<P>So do the car makers and every trade publication I've read in the last how ever many years.<P>There's no way on earth I'd put it in my car or anyone elses.<P>The entire brake system must be purged of Glycol based DOT3&4 fluid. The 2 are not compatible.<P>Presently I'm installing Valvoline Synthetic which has a higher wet(343F) and dry(502F) boil than 3-4. (DOT 3 wet boil is about 280) Yet it's still compatible with them and won't allow water slugs to form. All you need is to blow the lines out with clean dry compressed gas. A little pressure regulated CO2 or Nitrogen is perfect for that job. Basicly anything clean dry and petrolium free will work.||||reg||1
